hat we're using as a  starting point for discussion is what  did really bad years of flu and rsv and  other viral respiratory disease look  like pre-pandemic and using that sort of  as a benchmark for starting the  conversation i think some places will  want to really suppress cases even more  than that but some places might want to  be more liberal but that's a starting  place yeah some people say just stop  being afraid and just go back to work  and you're saying that's not at all what  you mean yeah i think i'm so tired of  being tired and being afraid of this i  am trying to navigate myself what can we  do what should we not do right and i  think the expression learn to live with  covid can be problematic because i think  it's really about saying to people stop  being scared get back to work that's not  what you're saying yeah what we're  saying is we're going to have to  co-exist with covid and how do we do  that safely where people are not ending  up in the hospital where our hospital  system is not buckling under the weight  of all of those coveted cases as it is  now can you can you help us understand  what's going on with kids right now  because we keep hearing about a big  increase in the number of kids  hospitalized and the total percentage of  kids hospitalized but at the same time  we're hearing that if you dig into the  numbers some of those kids are coming in  with a broken hand but they also test  positive so they're not there for coving  right so when we're kind of figuring out  risk and how bad things are how do we  deal with that  so we're seeing so much covid in the  community everybody who comes in the  hospital gets tested because we need to  sort out who needs to be in a covered  room versus a regular room so  everybody's getting a test and just  because there's so much of it out there  in the community you're going to have  some people who come in you know a kid  with a broken arm who just happens to  have copid and it is a challenge right  now they're you know i've just been on  service at bellevue the last two weeks  some of our patients and maybe i have a  patient who came in with heart failure  covet tipped them over the edge but they  don't really have a bad case of covid do  you say that's with or from you know  there's this whole gray zone in between  it makes it hard to know how worried you  should be then when you hear these  numbers  it is it is and this is something that  you know epidemiologists statisticians  are really trying to dig into to figure  out how much of this is truly omicron  causing bad lung disease versus you just  happen to have it dr gander so we got  vaccinated boosted so we can have  optimal protection right um and i want  to speak from the perspective of some  friends and family maybe even some  viewers that are sitting at home saying  okay well i know people that are  vaccinated i know people that are  boosted that are still getting coveted  um so why should i go out there and get  vaccinated and boost it because we still  have a large population of individuals  that are hesitant what would you say to  them that's right uh we have over a  third of americans who are still not  vaccinated and that is a real challenge  to getting back to a more close to  semblance of normal what i would say is  that yes we are seeing breakthrough  infections even in people who are  boosted but they're like the common cold  they're not landing you in the hospital  these are not people who are having to  put on a ventilator it's less severe  that doesn't work  that definition of mild though does seem  to be a slippery slope because i've  heard some people who who you guys say  are mild they're not in the hospital but  they're really really in a lot of  trouble and very and it's very painful  for them  the way we as doctors define what is  mild versus severe is really a question  of do you need oxygen do we need to put  you on supplemental oxygen have your  oxygen levels dropped without it tha